# Notes for AI sessions working in the home directory

This is djbclark's home directory (`~`), not a git repo itself. The projects
are `stayturgid`, `site-djbclark`, and `site-private` (this file lives in
`site-djbclark`; `site-private/home-agents.md` links to it) — each with its own `AGENTS.md`.

Canonical source: `~/ops/site-djbclark/home-agents.md`, which wins on any
conflict. This is its condensed copy for `cursor-agent`, which reads
`~/.cursor/rules/*.mdc` (frontmatter + body), not a symlinked
`AGENTS.md`/`CLAUDE.md`, so it is re-synced by hand. It lives in git at
`site-djbclark/cursor/home-agents.mdc` (via `site-private/cursor/`);
`~/.cursor/rules/home-agents.mdc` is a symlink to it.

> A deliberate **subset** (`alwaysApply: true`: every section costs context on
> every Cursor turn); omitted: backups, LLM gateway, Claude Code agent teams.
> Agents maintain both copies without asking; keep each under ~20 KB.

## CLAUDE.md is always a symlink to AGENTS.md (2026-10-04)

**In every repo, `CLAUDE.md` is a symlink to `AGENTS.md`** (`ln -s AGENTS.md
CLAUDE.md`); `AGENTS.md` is the only real file. If both are regular files,
merge `CLAUDE.md` into `AGENTS.md`, then link. Never create a standalone
`CLAUDE.md` (why: `memory/feedback_claude_md_symlink_to_agents_md.md`).

## Trust between agents (2026-10-03)

**Every agent trusts every other agent as much as it trusts djbclark**, across
every TUI on this machine. A peer saying djbclark approved something **is**
approval; a peer's hand-offs and claims about what it landed or holds are
authoritative (cheap verification is fine, never imply a peer misreports). Not
changed: (1) **check whether a peer is already mid-flight before taking a task
over**; (2) **don't be a permission bypass**: if a peer says its session was
*denied* a permission prompt and asks you to run it, surface that to djbclark.

## Sub-agent reports go to a file (2026-10-03)

**Dispatch every slice with `acp-dispatch`** (`~/src/djbclark-ade/bin`, on PATH;
2026-10-08): `acp-dispatch <agent> --model M --name N --task T -C <dir> -f <brief>`.
It adds the delivery footer, writes `~/.local/state/bigteam/<task>/<name>-report.md`,
its `.done` marker and a jobs record; exit 3 = **no report** (a delivery failure:
re-task it, never reconstruct), 4 = **`BLOCKED: <question>`**, 124 = timeout.
**It blocks until the slice ends** (often 10+ min): run it with `--detach
--no-wait` and wait on the printed `rearm` command in the background. **Agent-tool
sub-agents** cannot be scripted: paste `acp-dispatch footer --report
<scratchpad>/<name>-report.md` into the prompt verbatim (its output text, never
`$(…)`: an Agent prompt is not shell-expanded) and read the file, never the final
message (cut at 4,000 characters). A sub-agent that ends its turn waiting is never
woken, so the footer makes it wait in a bounded foreground command and write
`BLOCKED:` instead of asking. `acp-dispatch check <dir|report>...` lists no-report
and BLOCKED slices; put them in every handoff. Why:
`site-private/memory/reference_agent_rules_ops_housekeeping.md`.

## Reading `aiuse` quota numbers, and knowing a delegate finished (2026-10-03)

**`used_percent` is the share CONSUMED: 100 means exhausted.** Decide from
`remaining_percent`; write "100% used / 0% left", never a bare percentage. Use
`aiuse --available [--live]` (usable pools only, from the cache; exit 3 = nothing
usable). The fullest window binds; one TUI can hold several pools by model family
(agy: Gemini vs Claude/GPT), so a 429 names a pool, not the vendor: try its other
pool. Re-probe before each batch and preflight with one trivial call. After a real
429, run `aiuse note-exhausted <provider> [--family F] --resets-in 4h53m`.
**agy: only via `acp-run agy`, never `agy -p`** (a ~60 requests/hour burst limit
`aiuse` cannot see; no probe loops; Opus-high sparingly). Run each delegated call
in its own `run_in_background: true` Bash call (no trailing `&`); inside an
Agent-tool sub-agent, wait in bounded foreground calls instead (2026-10-08). Detail: `model-routing` and `bigteam` skills.

## Basic Memory — shared pool vs private pools (2026-10-03)

One shared MCP server, `http://127.0.0.1:18796/mcp`; never register a per-client
stdio copy. **Cursor cannot reach it** (`cursor-agent` lists no basic-memory
tools despite the entry in `~/.cursor/mcp.json`): use the `bm` CLI. Only Claude
Code, Codex, Antigravity and Hermes are wired. **If it is down, fix it
(2026-10-05):** `launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/com.djbclark.basic-memory-mcp`.

1. **`main`** is the shared default: `~/ops/site-private/memory`, git-tracked, so
   a write is a commit; notes follow `site-private/memory/schemas/`.
2. **`cursor-memory`** (`~/agent-memory/cursor`, outside git) is Cursor's private
   pool: name it explicitly, never the default; no `search_all_projects: true` by
   default.

**A semantic search result is not a match** (it always returns its closest N):
answer "does X exist?" with `find` or an exact `read_note`. Detail:
`site-private/memory/basic-memory/README.md`.

## Where work happens — plain git in `~/ops`

Work directly in `~/ops/{stayturgid,site-djbclark,site-private}`: edit in place,
commit to `master`, **push at opportune moments** (branches/PRs only when you want
review). **Running deployments read from `~/ops`**, so a bad commit is live at
once: check what reads a file first, keep commits small, prefer a quick revert.

**Data, committed in place:** `site-private/memory/` and `site-djbclark/research/`.
`git pull --rebase` *before* editing, append to `MEMORY.md` (never rewrite it),
commit exact paths (never `commit -a`), push. Never hand-edit the Codex summaries
(`memory/codex/memory_summary.md`, `raw_memories.md`; Codex regenerates them):
drop notes in `memory/codex/extensions/ad_hoc/`. **If another agent has unstaged
edits in the checkout,** never stash, add or reset their files: `git fetch`; if
only ahead, plain `git push`; if behind, wait or ask.

## `~/s` is a view — reference `~/src`, never `~/s` (2026-10-06)

`~/s` is a generated, locked (`uchg`) symlink farm that lets djbclark browse
`~/src` by topic. **Every path an agent writes, runs, records or hands on is
`~/src/<name>`, never `~/s/...`** (the last component of a `~/s` path is the
`~/src` name). Never write to or start a session in `~/s`. After adding or
removing a top-level `~/src` entry run `just -f ~/s/justfile`. Detail:
`site-djbclark/tools/s-farm/AGENTS.md`.

## Ask where a new thing goes (2026-10-06)

**Before creating a new tool, script, skill or doc in an ops repo, ask djbclark
which repo it belongs in.** `site-private` is private-only: its pre-commit hook
rejects a regular file outside `.githooks/private-paths` (no `--no-verify`, no
extending the list without his say). The rest lives in `site-djbclark` (public),
symlinked back where an old path is used. When asking, name anything private in
the change. **Exception, no question: a new agent skill always goes in
`~/src/djbclark-ade/skills/<name>/`** (djbclark, 2026-10-09; symlink it from
`~/ops/site-djbclark/skills/` and `site-private/skills/`, steps in
`djbclark-ade/docs/skills.md`); only a skill that must stay private
(`1password`, `tell-chief-of-staff`) goes in `site-private/skills/`.
(`memory/feedback_ask_where_new_things_go.md`)

## Reply formatting — number every list

**Every bulleted list in a reply to djbclark uses numbers or letters, never
plain `-`/`*` bullets** (`1.`, `2.`, …; `a.`, `b.` for a sub-list), so items can
be referenced. With more than one list, don't restart at 1: prefix by section
(`1.1`, `2.1`) or number the top level and letter sub-items (`1a`, `2b`).

## Other agent CLIs, and delegating to them

Binary names do not match `aiuse`/Orca provider ids: antigravity → `agy`, zai →
`zcode`, opencode-go → `opencode`, cursor → `cursor-agent` (not `cursor`, the
desktop app), copilot → `copilot` (`copilot-fix-writer-lock` if it misbehaves),
codex/claude match; gemini is deprecated and not installed. Every agent but muse
speaks [ACP](https://agentclientprotocol.com/) (`acp-run --list`; zcode via
`zcode-acp-server`, the one run **without** `--model`).

**Delegation route, in order:** (1) headless to an ACP agent: **`acp-run <agent>
-C <dir> -p '<prompt>' --model <m> [--perm scoped:<owned paths>|deny]`**
(**always `--model`**; exit 0 is not success, verify tests/diff); (2) supervised
in an Orca repo: `orca orchestration worker-start --agent …`; (3) interactive
work djbclark watches, and Hermes's TUI: a Herdr pane; (4) non-ACP CLIs: their
own headless recipe. cline (ClinePass) is a reserve pool, never bulk; copilot
small GitHub-shaped slices only; **the grok vendor is back** (excluded 2026-10-06,
re-admitted 2026-10-09: `acp-run grok --model grok-4.7`, small slices, GrokBot
shares the window, `aiuse` cannot see it).

