## Context size: prompt early, delegate by default (2026-10-08)

**Keep context small: by default send discrete, separable tasks to `bigteam`**
(or a sub-agent) so their reads never land here. At ~40k tokens of growth since
the session's start, delegate from then on and name the options once; past ~70k
(or 150k total), **ask djbclark at the next natural boundary: `/compact`,
`/handoff` then a new session, or continue and delegate.** In a herdr pane or an
Orca terminal, compact yourself there instead:
`~/src/djbclark-ade/bin/self-slash "/compact <focus>"`. It refuses while anything
is on his input line; then ask him to type it, never send over a draft. Another
session may be running bigteam (herdr tab `coord`): never touch its claimed files
or panes (bigteam Step 0).

## Fix the cause of a tool-calling mistake (2026-10-06)

**When you make a tool-calling mistake of a kind that can recur**, **fix the
instructions that would have prevented it in the same turn, unasked**: the
skill that covers it, this file, or a memory note. Say what you changed.
(`memory/feedback_fix_instructions_after_tool_mistake.md`)

## Ping djbclark on Hermes (2026-10-05)

**When a task completes or something needs djbclark's attention** (blocked, a
decision, a failure), also send it to the Hermes Telegram Inbox:
`~/.local/bin/hermes-ping "<repo>: <one line>"`, never bare `hermes send`: it adds
the interface, workspace/tab/pane, session and a focus link. One line per event,
never a loop. Operator notices from scripts go to Hermes too, not macOS
notifications. (`memory/feedback_ping_telegram_and_chat.md`)

## Periodic updates are a script, not a cron prompt (2026-10-05)

**When djbclark asks for periodic updates ("report every N minutes", "keep me
posted"), do not schedule a recurring model prompt** (CronCreate, /loop). Use
`~/ops/site-private/bin/fleet-watch` (launchd, every 5 min, Hermes notice only on
change; `fleet-watch status`), or extend it / write a similar script. Tell him
results as work finishes or fails. A one-shot scheduled check is fine.
(`memory/feedback_periodic_updates_by_script.md`)

## Heavy builds and tests: run them through `bg` (2026-10-04)

Run builds and tests through `~/ops/site-private/bin/bg` (`bg pytest`, `bg
gradlew …`; **always the full path, bare `bg` is the shell builtin**). **A test
command (pytest, tox, nox, `run_tests*.py`) must go through bg**: it caps
parallelism (`BG_CPUS`=3) and takes one of 2 machine-wide test slots. Run changed
files first (`--lf -x`), the full suite only when asked. **Never `taskpolicy -b`
for builds** (25x slower under load). Never throttle or SIGSTOP another session's
processes: its shell moves on and the harness kills the tree.

**Gradle: one build at a time, machine-wide (2026-10-06).** Check `pgrep -fl
GradleWrapperMain` first; "Waiting for the machine-wide Gradle slot" means queued,
not hung: don't kill or retry it (`~/.local/state/gradle-slot/STATUS`). Limits
(2026-10-05): profiles in `~/ops/site-private/gradle/profiles/` (default `low`: 2
workers, JVMs see 2 CPUs, low priority; `night` only with an expiry via
`bin/gradle-limits set night --until 07:00`), linked into every Gradle home. Don't
override with `--max-workers` or `-Dorg.gradle.*`; `bg` waits while the load per
core is over 1.5. Detail: `docs/gradle-limits.md`.

## Waiting on background work, and reading results

Wait on a file (`until [ -s f ]`) or a PID (`while kill -0 pid`), never
`pgrep -f '<pattern>'`: the waiting shell matches its own pattern. Never
`pkill -f '<pattern>'` either: it kills every shell whose command line holds the
pattern, your own wrapper included (2026-10-10). Kill the saved PID.
**Never read a command's result through a pipe** (`cmd 2>&1 | tail`): the exit
status is the last stage's. Write to a file, print the status, then read:
`cmd > out.log 2>&1; echo "rc=$?"; tail -5 out.log`.
(`memory/feedback_exit_status_through_pipe.md`)

## Code discovery — symbol tools before `cat`/`rg` (2026-10-03)

**`graft` is the navigator in every repo** (`graft build` first if it has no
`graft/`; its footprint is djbclark's call, not a concern). Use a symbol lookup
before `cat`/`rg`/Grep for **code discovery**; plain text search is right for
prose and config. `token-savior` is kept only for what graft cannot do
(`find_dead_code`, `detect_breaking_changes`, `analyze_config`,
`find_semantic_duplicates`, index-aware edits), never as a navigator; `rtk` owns
Bash output compaction, so **never run `ts init`**. Defects and the registration
list: `site-djbclark/docs/tooling-policy.md`.

## Run commands yourself (2026-10-06)

Run every command yourself, including Touch ID / `sudo-ask` steps; never tell
djbclark to type a `!` command. Privileged steps still use their sanctioned path.

## Tools and habits (2026-10-06)

1. **Fast tools:** search with `rg`, **always with a path** (`rg PAT .`;
   2026-10-09; `rtk rg` is real ripgrep, `rtk grep` is BSD grep), list files with
   `rg --files`/`fd`, code structure with `ast-grep`, `uv` not `pip`, **literal
   replace: `srgn -L --fail-none --stdin-detection force-unreadable --glob FILE 'FIND' -- 'REPL'`**
   (2026-10-09; FILE is relative to the cwd, an absolute `--glob` matches
   nothing; FIND spans lines as typed, **exit 1 when nothing matched**,
   `--dry-run` previews; a FIND starting with `-` goes through the Edit tool;
   **REPL turns backslash escapes into control characters** (`\b` became a
   backspace, 2026-10-10): REPL with `\` goes through Python with an assert).
   **`sd` is retired for edits** (exit 0 on no match; it lost an edit).
   `dust`/`procs`/`xh` where they fit. Keep `cat`/`ls`/`diff`/`jq` (rtk compacts
   them). Android greps stay `grep`. (`memory/feedback_modern_cli_tools.md`)
2. **Replace shared scripts atomically** (temp file, then `mv -f`), never edit in
   place (a running bash dies). (`memory/feedback_edit_running_scripts_atomically.md`)
3. **Default browser:** open pages with `open <url>` (Orion), not Chrome.
   (`memory/feedback_always_use_default_browser_orion.md`)
4. **"All agents" includes Hermes**, which `aiuse` doesn't list.
   (`memory/feedback_all_agents_includes_hermes.md`)
5. **New tool, MCP server, skill or plugin added anywhere? Ask djbclark whether to
   add it to `djbclark-ade/docs/apply-toolchain-prompt.md`** (2026-10-09).
   (`memory/feedback_ask_update_apply_toolchain_prompt.md`)
6. **Never pass `--help` to a subcommand of a live-service CLI** (2026-10-09:
   `collie update --help` started a real update). `collie` is read-only for agents
   (`collie version`, `collie help`, docs); `update`/`pair`/config changes are the
   operator's, queued with the command. (`memory/feedback_no_help_flag_on_live_service_cli.md`)

## Clipboard — never bare `pbcopy` (2026-10-03)

**"copy to clipboard"/"pbcopy" means `~/ops/site-private/bin/clip`**, never bare
`pbcopy` (agent shells have `LC_CTYPE=C`, so `—` pastes as `‚Äî`). `pbpaste`
cannot verify a `pbcopy` (`clip` does). Human-bound prose gets `clip --unwrap`,
no Markdown. **Every prompt you write for djbclark to hand to another agent also
goes to the clipboard via `clip`, unasked** (2026-10-06). Never overwrite the
pasteboard to test: save and restore it.

## Long documents — query the book KB, never paste the book (2026-10-03)

Books live in `~/ops/site-private/bin/book-kb`. Query cheapest-first:
`book-kb query '<regex>' <slug>` (exact; the only proof a term is or isn't in the
book), then `book-kb toc <slug>` (`--deep` for `file:line` anchors) and one
chapter. basic-memory `search_notes(project="books", …)` is semantic: a hit is
not a match. **Never read `~/kb/raw/<slug>.md`** (the whole book). Query
`learning-cfengine` (CFEngine 3.12) before writing or reviewing CFEngine policy.
Prefer a book's EPUB over its PDF. Detail:
`site-private/docs/book-kb-vs-book-to-skill.md`.

## Never waste spent tokens without asking (2026-09-30)

**Don't throw away work already paid for without asking djbclark first**: no
stopping a running agent partway, restarting from scratch, or rerunning something
whose result exists. When a pause is requested, stop scheduling new work and let
running work finish. If losing in-flight work seems worth it, explain the
trade-off and ask.

## Agents run in yolo (auto-approve) mode by default (2026-10-03)

**Every agent, however launched, runs in its yolo-or-equivalent mode** unless a
stated reason gates it (review-only slices, bigteam's `--perm scoped:`). Flags:
the `model-routing` skill. Yolo does not skip hooks, file-ownership rules, reserve
pools, or asking before hard-to-reverse actions. Detail:
`site-private/memory/feedback_yolo_default_for_all_agents.md`.

## Session logs (Tier 1 handoff pointers)

At the start of a session in any git repo, read its Tier 1 pointer (under
`~/.local/state/handoffs/`), compare `head_sha` to `HEAD`, and state a resume
plan before acting. Only the workspace-owning session writes it; a missing
pointer is a fresh start. Protocol:
`~/src/djbclark-ade/skills/session-handoff/SKILL.md`.

## Three-way memory/docs policy (start at each repo's AGENTS.md)

No single canonical copy — read all three: `stayturgid/AGENTS.md`,
`site-djbclark/AGENTS.md`, `site-private/AGENTS.md`.

## Research outward first (2026-10-05)

**Whenever you are spending, or about to spend, significant tokens on something a
web search might answer, search first**: how a tool, library, protocol, platform
or error behaves, including other projects' source and issue trackers, before
trial and error, device experiments, faking another system, or rounds of local
grepping. Then verify locally.
